6. Did the score separate them?
==============================================================================
8 actives against 8 property-matched decoys
  AUC-ROC  0.641 [0.317, 0.900]   (0.5 = random)
  EF 1%    2.00   (1.0 = random, ceiling 2.0)
  EF 5%    2.00
  BEDROC   0.920

  VERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE. The AUC interval spans 0.58 (0.317 to 0.900), reaching from worse than random to good. This screen is too small to settle the question either way.
  This is NOT evidence that docking fails here. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, and reporting it as such would be the error this pipeline exists to avoid.
  To narrow the interval to 0.20 would take roughly 68 compounds per group, against the 8 used here.
  Note: the top 1% is 1 compound(s), so EF1% here moves by a large factor on a single result. Read the AUC interval instead.

  mean score: actives -9.03, decoys -8.35 kcal/mol (difference -0.68)

==============================================================================
Verdict
==============================================================================
Docking does NOT demonstrably separate actives from matched decoys on BACE1 (AUC 0.641 [0.317, 0.900], reaching below random). On this evidence it should not be used to triage candidates here at all.
This is a finding, not a failure. Docking enrichment fails on many targets, and the alternative -- a ranked candidate list ordered by a score that carries no signal -- is worse than no list.

